window.docContents[169].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Corollary-2___3___10-is-false-over-the-reals/#Chapter2___Remark2___3___11":{"contents":"Remark 2.3.11. Note that this corollary is false over the field of real numbers: it suffices to take A = \\mathbb{C} (regarded as an \\mathbb{R}-algebra) and V = A.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.3. Representations\u0009Corollary 2.3.10 is false over the reals","header":"Corollary 2.3.10 is false over the reals","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Corollary-2___3___10-is-false-over-the-reals/#Chapter2___Remark2___3___11"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Left-and-right-modules-over-commutative-rings/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___3___-Representations--Left-and-right-modules-over-commutative-rings":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.3. Representations","header":"Left and right modules over commutative rings","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Left-and-right-modules-over-commutative-rings/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___3___-Representations--Left-and-right-modules-over-commutative-rings"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Universal-enveloping-algebra-of-the-Heisenberg-Lie-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___9___-Lie-algebras--Universal-enveloping-algebra-of-the-Heisenberg-Lie-algebra--Universal-enveloping-algebra-of-the-Heisenberg-Lie-algebra--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"An algebra equivalence between the displayed algebras.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Example2.9.13; role=primary]\n\nBoth displayed properties hold.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Example2.9.13; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.9. Lie algebras\u0009Universal enveloping algebra of the Heisenberg Lie algebra\u0009Universal enveloping algebra of the Heisenberg Lie algebra\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Universal-enveloping-algebra-of-the-Heisenberg-Lie-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___9___-Lie-algebras--Universal-enveloping-algebra-of-the-Heisenberg-Lie-algebra--Universal-enveloping-algebra-of-the-Heisenberg-Lie-algebra--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___10___-Representations-of-tensor-products/Section-3___10___-Representations-of-tensor-products-___-heading-and-setup/#Chapter3___Introduction_to_3___10___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.10. Representations of tensor products\u0009Section 3.10: Representations of tensor products — heading and setup\u0009Section 3.10: Representations of tensor products — heading and setup","header":"Formalization","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___10___-Representations-of-tensor-products/Section-3___10___-Representations-of-tensor-products-___-heading-and-setup/#Chapter3___Introduction_to_3___10___formalization"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___2___-The-density-theorem/The-density-theorem/#Chapter3___Theorem3___2___2":{"contents":"Theorem 3.2.2 (The density theorem). (i) Let V be an irreducible finite dimensional representation of A. Then the map \\rho : A \\to \\operatorname{End} V is surjective.\n\n(ii) Let V = V_1 \\oplus \\cdots \\oplus V_r, where V_i are irreducible pairwise nonisomorphic finite dimensional representations of A. Then the map \\bigoplus_{i=1}^r \\rho_i : A \\to \\bigoplus_{i=1}^r \\operatorname{End}(V_i) is surjective.In general, it is better to consider direct products rather than direct sums of algebras; for example, the direct product of any collection of unital algebras is unital, while the direct sum is not if the collection is infinite. However, we will only consider finite direct sums, in which case this distinction is immaterial.\n\nProof. (i) Let B be the image of A in \\operatorname{End}(V). We want to show that B = \\operatorname{End}(V). Let c \\in \\operatorname{End}(V), let v_1, \\ldots, v_n be a basis of V, and let w_i = cv_i. By Corollary 3.2.1, there exists a \\in A such that av_i = w_i. Then a maps to c, so c \\in B, and we are done.\n\n(ii) Let B_i be the image of A in \\operatorname{End}(V_i), and let B be the image of A in \\bigoplus_{i=1}^r \\operatorname{End}(V_i). Recall that as a representation of A, \\bigoplus_{i=1}^r \\operatorname{End}(V_i) is semisimple: it is isomorphic to \\bigoplus_{i=1}^r d_i V_i, where d_i = \\dim V_i. Then by Proposition 3.1.4, B = \\bigoplus_i B_i. On the other hand, (i) implies that B_i = \\operatorname{End}(V_i). Thus (ii) follows. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.2. The density theorem\u0009The density theorem","header":"The density theorem","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___2___-The-density-theorem/The-density-theorem/#Chapter3___Theorem3___2___2"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___2___-The-density-theorem/The-density-theorem/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___2___-The-density-theorem--The-density-theorem--The-density-theorem--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"For a finite-dimensional simple module over an algebraically closed field, the algebra action\nis surjective.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Corollary3.2.1/Derived2; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Discussion_proof_of_Theorem3.10.2; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Introduction_to_3.2; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Theorem3.2.2; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Theorem3.2.2/Derived2; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Theorem3.2.2/Derived4; role=supporting]\n\nFor a finite family of pairwise inequivalent finite-dimensional simple modules, the displayed\nfamily of algebra actions is surjective.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Introduction_to_3.2; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Theorem3.2.2; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Theorem3.2.2/Derived4; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Theorem3.5.4/Derived3; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Theorem3.6.2/Derived2; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.2. The density theorem\u0009The density theorem\u0009The density theorem\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___2___-The-density-theorem/The-density-theorem/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___2___-The-density-theorem--The-density-theorem--The-density-theorem--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___5___-Finite-dimensional-algebras/Rad_LPAR_A_RPAR_-is-the-largest-nilpotent-two-sided-ideal/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___5___-Finite-dimensional-algebras--Rad_LPAR_A_RPAR_-is-the-largest-nilpotent-two-sided-ideal":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.5. Finite dimensional algebras","header":"Rad(A) is the largest nilpotent two-sided ideal","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___5___-Finite-dimensional-algebras/Rad_LPAR_A_RPAR_-is-the-largest-nilpotent-two-sided-ideal/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___5___-Finite-dimensional-algebras--Rad_LPAR_A_RPAR_-is-the-largest-nilpotent-two-sided-ideal"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/Scalar-extension-and-the-Noether-Deuring-theorem/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem--Scalar-extension-and-the-Noether-Deuring-theorem--Scalar-extension-and-the-Noether-Deuring-theorem--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"An equivalence between positive finite function modules yields an equivalence between their value modules.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.8.4; role=supporting]\n\nThe displayed split-map existence statement follows from the same existence statement under the given hypotheses.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.8.4/Derived6; role=supporting]\n\nGiven the displayed tensor-product-module equivalence, there exists a finitely generated\nsubalgebra over which an equivalence exists.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.8.4; role=supporting]\n\nGiven the displayed tensor-product-module retraction, there exists a finitely generated\nsubalgebra over which a retraction exists.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.8.4/Derived6; role=supporting]\n\nThe scalar-extended module is linearly equivalent to functions indexed by the dimension of the extending field.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.8.4; role=supporting]\n\nAn equivalence after tensor extension yields an equivalence before tensor extension under finite-dimensionality hypotheses.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.8.4; role=supporting]\n\nA retraction after tensor extension yields a retraction before tensor extension under finite-dimensionality hypotheses.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.8.4/Derived6; role=supporting]\n\nA retraction after tensor extension yields a retraction before tensor extension under finite-dimensionality hypotheses.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.8.4; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.8. The Krull-Schmidt theorem\u0009Scalar extension and the Noether-Deuring theorem\u0009Scalar extension and the Noether-Deuring theorem\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/Scalar-extension-and-the-Noether-Deuring-theorem/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem--Scalar-extension-and-the-Noether-Deuring-theorem--Scalar-extension-and-the-Noether-Deuring-theorem--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___11___-Examples/Examples-of-induced-representations-for-S_3-and-S_4/#Chapter5___Discussion_5___11_examples":{"contents":"(1) Let G = S_3, H = \\mathbb{Z}_2. Using the Frobenius reciprocity, we obtain \\operatorname{Ind}_H^G \\mathbb{C}_+ = \\mathbb{C}^2 \\oplus \\mathbb{C}_+ and \\operatorname{Ind}_H^G \\mathbb{C}_- = \\mathbb{C}^2 \\oplus \\mathbb{C}_-.\n\n(2) Let G = S_3, H = \\mathbb{Z}_3. Then we obtain \\operatorname{Ind}_H^G \\mathbb{C}_+ = \\mathbb{C}_+ \\oplus \\mathbb{C}_-, \\operatorname{Ind}_H^G \\mathbb{C}_\\epsilon = \\operatorname{Ind}_H^G \\mathbb{C}_{\\epsilon^2} = \\mathbb{C}^2.\n\n(3) Let G = S_4, H = S_3. Then \\operatorname{Ind}_H^G \\mathbb{C}_+ = \\mathbb{C}_+ \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^3_-, \\operatorname{Ind}_H^G \\mathbb{C}_- = \\mathbb{C}_- \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^3_+, \\operatorname{Ind}_H^G \\mathbb{C}^2 = \\mathbb{C}^2 \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^3_- \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^3_+.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.11. Examples\u0009Examples of induced representations for S_3 and S_4","header":"Examples of induced representations for S_3 and S_4","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___11___-Examples/Examples-of-induced-representations-for-S_3-and-S_4/#Chapter5___Discussion_5___11_examples"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___13___-Proof-of-the-classification-theorem-for-representations-of-___S_n___/Proof-of-Lemma-5___13___4-using-idempotent-decomposition/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___13___-Proof-of-the-classification-theorem-for-representations-of-___S_n___--Proof-of-Lemma-5___13___4-using-idempotent-decomposition":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.13. Proof of the classification theorem for representations of $S_n$","header":"Proof of Lemma 5.13.4 using idempotent decomposition","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___13___-Proof-of-the-classification-theorem-for-representations-of-___S_n___/Proof-of-Lemma-5___13___4-using-idempotent-decomposition/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___13___-Proof-of-the-classification-theorem-for-representations-of-___S_n___--Proof-of-Lemma-5___13___4-using-idempotent-decomposition"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___14___-Induced-representations-for-___S_n___/Proof-of-Proposition-5___14___1/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___14___-Induced-representations-for-___S_n___--Proof-of-Proposition-5___14___1":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.14. Induced representations for $S_n$","header":"Proof of Proposition 5.14.1","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___14___-Induced-representations-for-___S_n___/Proof-of-Proposition-5___14___1/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___14___-Induced-representations-for-___S_n___--Proof-of-Proposition-5___14___1"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___14___-Induced-representations-for-___S_n___/Section-5___14___-Induced-representations-for-S_n-and-definition-of-U_lambda/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___14___-Induced-representations-for-___S_n___--Section-5___14___-Induced-representations-for-S_n-and-definition-of-U_lambda":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.14. Induced representations for $S_n$","header":"Section 5.14: Induced representations for S_n and definition of U_lambda","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___14___-Induced-representations-for-___S_n___/Section-5___14___-Induced-representations-for-S_n-and-definition-of-U_lambda/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___14___-Induced-representations-for-___S_n___--Section-5___14___-Induced-representations-for-S_n-and-definition-of-U_lambda"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___16___-Problems/Diagonalizability-of-_LPAR_12_RPAR_________________LPAR_1n_RPAR_-and-rectangular-Young-diagrams/#Chapter5___Problem5___16___3___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.16. Problems\u0009Diagonalizability of (12)+...+(1n) and rectangular Young diagrams\u0009Diagonalizability of (12)+...+(1n) and rectangular Young diagrams","header":"Formalization","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___16___-Problems/Diagonalizability-of-_LPAR_12_RPAR_________________LPAR_1n_RPAR_-and-rectangular-Young-diagrams/#Chapter5___Problem5___16___3___formalization"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/Completion-of-proof-of-Theorem-5___4___6/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem--Completion-of-proof-of-Theorem-5___4___6--Completion-of-proof-of-Theorem-5___4___6--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"A finite group element with conjugacy class of positive prime-power size admits a\nnontrivial simple complex representation whose dimension is not divisible by that prime and\nwhose character does not vanish at the element.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5.4.6; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Lemma5.4.7; role=primary]\n\nA finite group with a conjugacy class of positive prime-power size has a nontrivial proper\nnormal subgroup.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5.4.3; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5.4.6; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Theorem5.4.6; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.4. Burnside's theorem\u0009Completion of proof of Theorem 5.4.6\u0009Completion of proof of Theorem 5.4.6\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/Completion-of-proof-of-Theorem-5___4___6/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem--Completion-of-proof-of-Theorem-5___4___6--Completion-of-proof-of-Theorem-5___4___6--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___5___-Representable-functors/#chapter-07___section-7-5":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories","header":"7.5. Representable functors","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___5___-Representable-functors/#chapter-07___section-7-5"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___8___-Complexes-and-cohomology/Long-exact-sequence-of-cohomology/#Chapter7___Problem7___8___5":{"contents":"Problem 7.8.5. Let D_\\bullet be a complex of abelian groups with differentials d_i, i \\in \\mathbb{Z}, let C_\\bullet be a subcomplex of D_\\bullet (i.e. a collection of subgroups C_i \\subset D_i such that d_i(C_i) \\subset C_{i+1}), and let E_\\bullet = D_\\bullet / C_\\bullet be the quotient complex (i.e., E_i = D_i / C_i with differentials induced by d_i).\n\nDefine a homomorphism c_i : H^i(E) \\to H^{i+1}(C) as follows. Given x \\in H^i(E), pick a representative x' of x in E_i. Let x'' be a lift of x' to D_i. Let y' = dx'' \\in D_{i+1} (we abbreviate the notation, denoting d_i just by d). Since dx' = 0, y' = dx'' \\in C_{i+1}. Also, dy' = d^2 x'' = 0. So y' represents an element y \\in H^{i+1}(C). We will set c_i(x) = y.\n\n(i) Show that c_i is well defined, i.e., c_i(x) does not depend on the choice of x' and x''.\n\n(ii) Show that the sequence\n\n(7.8.1) \\ldots H^{i-1}(E) \\to H^i(C) \\to H^i(D) \\to H^i(E) \\to H^{i+1}(C) \\ldots,\n\nwhere the first map is c_{i-1}, the middle two maps are induced by the maps C_i \\to D_i \\to E_i, and the last map is c_i, is exact.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.8. Complexes and cohomology\u0009Long exact sequence of cohomology","header":"Long exact sequence of cohomology","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___8___-Complexes-and-cohomology/Long-exact-sequence-of-cohomology/#Chapter7___Problem7___8___5"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___4___-Homological-dimension/Homological-dimension-of-a-ring/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___4___-Homological-dimension--Homological-dimension-of-a-ring--Homological-dimension-of-a-ring--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"The third auxiliary extended-natural-valued ring invariant equals the second one for the\nopposite ring.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Definition9.4.3; role=supporting]\n\nAn auxiliary property of a ring indexed by a natural number.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Definition9.4.3; role=supporting]\n\nA second auxiliary property of a ring indexed by a natural number.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Definition9.4.3; role=supporting]\n\nA third auxiliary property of a ring indexed by a natural number.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Definition9.4.3; role=supporting]\n\nThe third auxiliary natural-number-indexed ring property is equivalent to the second one for\nthe opposite ring.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Definition9.4.3; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.4. Homological dimension\u0009Homological dimension of a ring\u0009Homological dimension of a ring\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___4___-Homological-dimension/Homological-dimension-of-a-ring/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___4___-Homological-dimension--Homological-dimension-of-a-ring--Homological-dimension-of-a-ring--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"}});